Z||000005||JSocha||12-19-2001||12:16 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Mark Matthews:<BR><B>Buddy at work saw it last night @ the midnight showing.  Said they did a really good job!  Said it's really good!  <I>It's 2:48 long tho!  Almost 3 hours!</I>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Well, it'll be time to load up the wife and kids, buy lots of popcorn as well as a donut pillow for any ol' hemroid flareups that might occur.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000006||MinnGreenGT||12-19-2001||12:25 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Voytek:<BR><B>Harry Potter thread watch out!!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Don't know if I should mention this (could be dangerous with this group)... I'm not trying to start or continue anything (I will <B>not</B> post anything in the Harry Potty thread... not my deal).<P>Anyway, a major difference between the two stories is this: Tolkien was a devout Catholic who was a good friend of C.S. Lewis (author of The Lion, the Witch, and the Wardrobe- & a highly reguarded religous writer).  It was Lewis who convinced Tolkien that a person could write fantasy without crossing any "religous lines". I don't believe the author of the HP series has that type of backing...<P>I love the Lord of the Rings stuff and I very much look forward to seeing it! Tolkien is so thorough that he created an entire world- literally from the beginning.  It is unbelievable the detail and complexity he attained.  Can you imagine what must've been floating around in his head?<P>As to Harry Potty, I'll probably see it when it comes out on video... I'm just not inspired to see it- and I don't believe that there is anything wrong with it.<P><B><FONT SIZE="1">Again... just dropping some useless information (yes, my head is full of it)- let's not let this thread get off topic or turn into a flame war!</FONT></B><P>------------------<BR>              <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/GreenGT6.gif"> ||||reg||14
Z||000007||johnt671||12-19-2001||12:37 PM||||I want to go see it Friday night.  The N.Y. papers gave it 3 1/2 to 4 stars.  ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Jaygee79||12-19-2001||12:38 PM||||i wanted to go see it today but i have 2 papers due this week  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">  maybe after christmas.||||reg||1
Z||000009||blakeinspace||12-23-2001||04:14 PM||||Saw it opening day and again on Sat.  I give it a 3.8L on a 4 star scale or a 4.9L on a 5 northstar scale.  Actually it was very good... but I never noticed that Liv Tyler had such a big overbite.  My friends reply to that was... "What?  You were looking at her face?"  Followed the book VERY closely, except for the opening synopsis with the forging of the one ring.  That info is spread over snippets and lore over the 3 book series.  And, most importantly, they did not include all of the random singing and poetry that goes on intermittently in Tolkien's books.  I like that stuff, but not in a movie.  Kinda like Ann Rice includes her husbands bad poetry in her books because he wouldn't be published any other way.  Actually I don't know where that comment was going... but I do highly recommend it for people that have not read the books... and those that have will find it very faithful.  Kinda like Harry Potter?  Please ignore that last sentence.<BR>-edit for spelling-<BR>------------------<BR>Even a stopped clock is right twice a day.<p>[This message has been edited by blakeinspace (edited 12-23-2001).]||||reg||7
Z||000010||johnt671||12-23-2001||05:56 PM||||Didn't get to go on Friday.  I got laid off Sept 8th, and the mail people have delivered my check that should have come Friday.  I've read those books about 20 times and hope to read them 20 more times.  I was a Marine in Vietnam, and I carried a poncho, poncho liner 3 bandoleers of ammo, three days of food, tooth brush and and at least two of the four books.  Read them when I could and I think they helped keep me partway sane.  I can't wait to see it.||||reg||1
Z||000011||lou_dias||12-23-2001||06:34 PM||||I didn't like where the movie ended.<BR>But I liked the movie...<BR>I'm borrowing the animated version that came out in 1978.  Some of the best animation I've ever seen.  They must have traced over real actors.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Mach10||12-23-2001||06:37 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by lou_dias:<BR><B>I didn't like where the movie ended.<BR>But I liked the movie...<BR>I'm borrowing the animated version that came out in 1978.  Some of the best animation I've ever seen.  They must have traced over real actors.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Have you read the books? The ending is fitting with the series. Remember, that the 3 parts constitute ONE book. Just think of it as the end of a chapter. The next one's due out in a year (  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> I want it NOW!)<P><BR>||||reg||7
Z||000013||lou_dias||12-23-2001||10:15 PM||||yeah, or atleast in a couple of months...||||reg||1
Z||000014||TRiAD||12-23-2001||11:03 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by MinnGreenGT:<BR><B>...Anyway, a major difference between the two stories is this: Tolkien was a devout Catholic who was a good friend of C.S. Lewis (author of The Lion, the Witch, and the Wardrobe- & a highly reguarded religous writer).  It was Lewis who convinced Tolkien that a person could write fantasy without crossing any "religous lines". I don't believe the author of the HP series has that type of backing...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>We were discussing this over dinner tonight. Someone was talkign about TLOTR and whether it is comparable to the Harry Potter movie. <P>Someone else said it was amazing how fast they copied off the idea of HP and must have found out about the movie in the early stages of production. (People do jump to conclusions, don't they?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> )<P>Of course, HP was a children's book, written for children, and possibly copying off of another author (a book about a warlock with a VERY similar name was written years ago), whereas Tolkein wrote the Lord of the Rings trilogy a VERY long time ago, and it was never targeted at children. (Neither were C.S. Lewis' "Chronicals of Narnia", the religious-themed fantasy books that include "The Lion, The Witch and the Wardrobe".)<P>The caliber of the writing and content are completely different, as are the target audiences. This "should" be evident in the films. <P>That said, I plan to see both, to see the special effects, and to see how close the LOTR movie came to the book from the series. <BR><p>[This message has been edited by TRiAD (edited 12-23-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000015||baptistheart||12-23-2001||11:11 PM||||I had the pleasure of reading the trilogy back when i was in high school ,and it is still my favorite set of books. I don't see the movie being as good, but ill give it a shot anyway.<P>I would highly recommend the books to anyone, but read the hobbit first.<P><BR>Oh and everyone have a merry christmas.<BR>------------------<BR>Joe<p>[This message has been edited by baptistheart (edited 12-23-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000016||Dave Gunsul||12-23-2001||11:54 PM||||I saw it and it was pretty good! Definitely worth seeing but you all might want to take the hemorroid cusion that JSocha is taking because it is a looong movie! My butt didn't wake up until we were almost all the way to the car.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>Activities Director<BR>Northern IL. Fiero Enthusiasts<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver||||reg||14
Z||000017||DXR_DAD||12-24-2001||12:26 AM||||Saw the movie last night and thought it was an excellent film.  As an avid D&D(Dungeons & Dragons) player I had a real appreciation for this film.  Heard of the books in high school but never had the chance to read them, know I regret it.  I would recommend that everyone check this movie out.||||reg||14
Z||000018||MinnGreenGT||12-24-2001||11:25 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by TRiAD:<BR><B>Someone else said it was amazing how fast they copied off the idea of HP and must have found out about the movie in the early stages of production. (People do jump to conclusions, don't they?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> )<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>LMAO, That's hilarious!!! People can be so ignorant about things like that.<P>People who haven't heard may be interested to find out that the entire story was filmed at one time- 18 months in New Zealand!<P>I saw the movie on Saturday night... awsome.  My sister-in-law was disappointed with the ending, but she had never read the books and was not warned in advance.  Quote while leaving the theater: "I really expected after sitting through 3 hours of that, that I'd at least get some resolution..."  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Had to laugh at that one. I took her shopping yesterday and she picked up a bridged edition of The lord of the rings on cassette tape (11 tapes?) so she could find out more about the story!<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>posted by lou_dias:<B><BR>yeah, or atleast in a couple of months...<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Sorry to say, but they have "The Two Towers" scheduled for release next December, and "Return of the King" to be released in December of 2003!  Sucks that it takes so long... maybe it'll get more poeple to read the books!||||reg||1
